# Web and Android Feature Scope Rules

## Intent
Define which surfaces the native Android (React Native) app carries, and how the web app covers
everything else.

**The test (owner decision, 2026-10-06): the Android app carries any plugin that materially benefits
from being an installed mobile app, plus whatever that plugin needs to run (sign-in, account, bug
reporting).** Everything else is served by the installable web app (PWA). Full 1:1 Android parity is
too much overhead and is not coming back. **There will never be an iOS app**; iPhone members use the
web app.

"Materially benefits" means the installed app can do something the web app cannot, or cannot do
well: keep live audio or video running with the screen off or another app in front (a foreground
service), or capture the phone's own screen (Android lets an installed app do this after asking; a
web page never can). A plugin that only shows lists, forms or posts does not qualify.

History: on 2026-07-20 the app was narrowed to Chyme alone, replacing the former "100% web↔Android
parity" mandate. Beacon was added under the test above on 2026-10-06: its repeated ask was sharing
the phone's screen in a live broadcast, which only an installed app can do. PeerProgramming was added
the same day: its live Session call keeps running with the screen off, and an installed app can share
the phone's screen in it. Foundation was added the same day for its instant calls only: an installed
app can ring the phone dependably with the app closed (native push) and keep a call going with the
screen off. When a plugin is added,
it gets a line in the keep-list below, a feature folder, a `plugin-parity-contracts.json` entry with
`requiresMobileSurface: true`, and a card in the app's Apps list (`src/features/apps/AppsList.tsx`).

## Why the change
The web app is now an installable PWA (`packages/web/app/manifest.ts` + `public/sw.js` + web push):
members "Add to Home screen" on Android or iOS and get a standalone app icon that opens the Commons
in its own window and receives push. So the mobile-responsive web app already covers the whole
product on phones. What the web cannot do well is live audio or video that keeps running in the
background, and capturing the phone's screen, so **that** is what the native app is for. Maintaining a second full
implementation of every plugin in React Native is no longer worth the cost.

## Android app scope — the keep-list (authoritative)
The native Android app opens on an **Apps list** (the plugins below, one card each) and carries ONLY:

1. **Clerk authentication** — sign-in / sign-up / session (`packages/mobile/src/auth/`). Required so a
   member can be signed in to use Chyme.
2. **Chyme** (live audio) — `packages/mobile/src/features/chyme/` and everything it links to. **Any
   feature reachable from the Chyme plugin (its header, its screens, the things it opens) stays in the
   app.** Its live-audio background behavior (foreground service so audio keeps playing when the app
   is backgrounded) is why it qualifies.
2b. **Beacon** (live broadcasts) — `packages/mobile/src/features/beacon/`. Watching (HLS), the live
   chat for signed-in members, and, for an admin, going live with the camera and microphone or by
   sharing the phone's screen. Screen sharing from a phone is why it qualifies.
2c. **PeerProgramming** (goal board, cohort chat, live Session call) —
   `packages/mobile/src/features/peer-programming/`. The goal board and the chat come with it so the
   member has the entire plugin in one place. It qualifies because the live Session call keeps running
   with the screen off or another app in front (the Stream foreground service), and on Android an
   installed app can share the phone's screen in that call.
2d. **Foundation** (instant 1:1 calls only) — `packages/mobile/src/features/foundation/`. Call alerts
   on this device (native push), the incoming-call ring with Answer / Decline, the live audio call with
   Mute, Extend and End, and "Connect now" from the member's connections. It qualifies because an
   installed app can ring the phone with the app closed and keep the call going with the screen off
   (the Stream foreground service). Every other Foundation screen — provider search, quotes, history,
   messages and the other settings — stays web-only.
3. **Bug reporting** — `packages/mobile/src/features/bug-reporting/`, so a member on the app can report
   a problem.
4. **Settings / account** — the account/settings surface (theme, sign-out, the shared top-actions the
   Chyme header shows).

Nothing else. If a surface is not on this list and is not required to keep a listed plugin running,
it does **not** belong in the Android app. It lives on the web app only. Adding a plugin is the
owner's decision, made against the test in Intent; an agent may propose one with the reason, never
add one unasked.

## What agents must and must not do
- **Do not build or maintain Android/React Native parity for any feature outside the keep-list.** New
  plugins, new plugin features, and changes to existing non-keep-list plugins are **web-only** now.
  Do not add a React Native screen, feature dir, API client, or `plugin-parity-contracts.json` entry
  for them.
- **Do** keep the keep-list surfaces working on Android. A change to Clerk auth, Chyme, bug reporting,
  or settings must still land its Android side, because those are the surfaces the app ships.
- If a web change to a non-keep-list feature happens to touch shared mobile infra that Chyme depends
  on (theme, `authedFetch`, `StreamChatView`, the shell), update that shared infra so Chyme keeps
  working — but do not resurrect the feature's own Android screen.
- Deprecated React Native feature code outside the keep-list is being **deleted** (owner decision:
  deleting is more honest than hiding — hidden screens rot, mislead the next agent, and still have to
  typecheck; git history and the web app preserve them). Do not re-add deleted screens without an
  explicit owner instruction to resume parity for that feature.

## Resuming parity later
The owner may later decide to resume Android parity for specific features ("maybe when there are a lot
of issues I can resume android parity outside of chyme"). That is an explicit, per-feature owner
decision — not a default. Until the owner says so for a named feature, treat that feature as web-only.

## Mobile-first web (owner decision, 2026-07-20): one phone-width layout at every viewport
- The web app (`packages/web`) ships a **single phone-width layout**. There is no separate desktop
  layout anymore, and **no agent builds, restores, or maintains one** — that maintenance burden is
  exactly what this decision removes.
- Desktop access remains: a wide viewport renders the **same** phone layout inside a centered
  phone-proportioned column (`.ctf-phone-frame` in `packages/web/app/globals.css`) — the mobile
  view, larger, with the root background filling the sides. Do not "fix" a desktop page back to a
  multi-column desktop layout; that is intentional, not a defect.
- The mechanism, so it is not accidentally undone:
  - `packages/web/hooks/use-is-mobile.ts` is **pinned to `true`** (server and client). Every shell
    that branches on it renders its phone branch everywhere.
  - The `.ctf-bp-mobile` / `.ctf-bp-desktop` helpers in `globals.css` always show the mobile
    wrapper and always hide the desktop wrapper.
  - The formerly phone-only `@media (max-width: …)` rules in the CSS modules were unwrapped to
    apply at every width (marked with a `Mobile-first:` comment where each block was).
- **New screens get one layout: the phone layout.** Do not add new `useIsMobile` desktop branches,
  new `.ctf-bp-desktop` markup, or new desktop-only media queries. Existing hidden desktop branches
  are dead code; deleting one when touching a shell is a welcome, non-gated refactor.
- The `Mobile*` design mockups remain the reference for the phone layout (and for the Chyme Android
  screens). A phone layout is the same whether the installed PWA, a ~390px browser, or the desktop
  phone frame paints it.
- Building the phone layout is an **app / front-end (non-design-agent) responsibility** and is
  **not** design-gated by rule 127.
- A web feature is "done" when its phone-width layout works — that single layout now serves every
  surface.

## Delivery Rules — the CI Parity Status line
- The CI parity gate (`pr-parity-status` in `ci.yml`) still requires the PR description to contain
  `Parity Status: web + mobile-responsive + android complete` **or** `Parity Ticket: #<issue>`.
- Under the mobile-first policy the "web" and "mobile-responsive" parts of that line are **one and
  the same deliverable**: the single phone-width layout. Shipping it satisfies both words. The line
  keeps its three-part wording only so the CI gate and older PRs stay stable — do not read it as a
  requirement to build a desktop layout.
- For a **web-only** change to a non-keep-list feature (the common case now), use
  `Parity Status: web + mobile-responsive + android complete`: the phone-width layout is the
  deliverable, and "android" is complete in the sense that **there is no Android surface for this
  feature** — the native app intentionally does not carry it. State "Android: out of scope
  (web-only per rule 105)" in the PR body so the reader knows it was a decision, not a forgotten gap.
- For a change to a **keep-list** surface (Clerk, Chyme, Beacon, PeerProgramming, Foundation calls, bug reporting, settings), the
  Android side is in scope and must land with it, exactly as before.

## Testing Rules
- Add acceptance criteria for critical paths in test plans. For Chyme specifically, the background/
  foreground-audio behavior (audio keeps playing when the app is backgrounded, member is not dropped)
  must be verified on a real device build — it cannot be verified from source review alone.
- Validate web accessibility and trauma-informed constraints when introducing new user workflows.

## No Phased Rollouts
- The narrowed scope is not a "phase 1 / phase 2" rollout. Non-keep-list features are web-only now;
  they are not "Android later." If the owner resumes parity for a named feature, that is a fresh,
  explicit decision, not a pre-planned phase. See `CLAUDE.md` → "Task Planning — No Phases".

## Prohibited
- Building Android/React Native implementations for features outside the keep-list.
- Re-adding deleted non-keep-list React Native screens without an explicit owner instruction.
- Marking a keep-list change complete when only the web side landed and the Android side was skipped.
- Using phase language to describe the narrowed scope.
